WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.060
This lab mystery uses the diode rectifier example in Circuit Studio Mastery.

2
00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.923
First establish what a healthy circuit does.

3
00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:12.152
Then use measurements to diagnose one hidden fault.

4
00:00:12.402 --> 00:00:18.184
This is a worked demonstration: pause before the diagnosis if you want to test your own

5
00:00:18.184 --> 00:00:18.545
explanation.

6
00:00:21.345 --> 00:00:25.601
The source drives a diode. a load resistor. and a smoothing capacitor.

7
00:00:25.851 --> 00:00:28.914
The diode passes the positive part of the input.

8
00:00:29.164 --> 00:00:33.366
The capacitor charges near the peak and supplies the load between peaks.

9
00:00:33.616 --> 00:00:39.036
A working smoothing capacitor therefore keeps the output from falling all the way to zero

10
00:00:39.036 --> 00:00:39.759
every cycle.

11
00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:45.663
In breadboard mode. open the fault challenge panel.

12
00:00:45.913 --> 00:00:50.159
Read the controls and start a challenge after checking the healthy circuit.

13
00:00:50.409 --> 00:00:55.336
For this demonstration. one reproducible open lead fault has been selected.

14
00:00:55.586 --> 00:01:00.893
Your own challenge can produce a different fault. so diagnose the evidence you actually

15
00:01:00.893 --> 00:01:01.272
observe.

16
00:01:04.072 --> 00:01:09.766
The input still oscillates. but the output now falls close to zero between positive peaks.

17
00:01:10.016 --> 00:01:13.182
Rectification remains. while smoothing is lost.

18
00:01:13.432 --> 00:01:18.742
This narrows the possibilities. but a waveform alone is not a complete diagnosis.

19
00:01:18.992 --> 00:01:24.446
Consider an open capacitor connection. a missing supply. and a problem in the load path.

20
00:01:27.246 --> 00:01:30.236
Turn power off before making a resistance measurement.

21
00:01:30.486 --> 00:01:32.058
Probe the load resistor.

22
00:01:32.308 --> 00:01:37.885
In this example. it reads about one kilo ohm. consistent with its nominal value.

23
00:01:38.135 --> 00:01:43.448
That supports the load resistor being present. although other connected paths can affect

24
00:01:43.448 --> 00:01:45.900
resistance measurements in a real circuit.

25
00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.856
Check continuity between the load return and ground.

26
00:01:52.106 --> 00:01:54.828
The return path is intact in this example.

27
00:01:55.078 --> 00:01:58.479
Combine this with the known input and the load resistance.

28
00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:03.596
The missing smoothing is now more consistent with the capacitor branch being disconnected

29
00:02:03.596 --> 00:02:05.842
than with a missing ground return.

30
00:02:08.642 --> 00:02:12.664
Select the suspected component and fault type in the challenge panel.

31
00:02:12.914 --> 00:02:16.818
Here the diagnosis is an open lead on capacitor C one.

32
00:02:17.068 --> 00:02:22.449
Explain that the input remains present. the output has lost smoothing. and the load and

33
00:02:22.449 --> 00:02:25.678
return checks are consistent with a working load path.

34
00:02:25.928 --> 00:02:27.816
Then submit the diagnosis.

35
00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:35.863
After the diagnosis is checked and the fault is repaired. inspect the waveform again.

36
00:02:36.113 --> 00:02:37.600
Smoothing should return.

37
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:42.884
A repair is supported by the restored circuit behavior. not just a success message.

38
00:02:43.134 --> 00:02:45.036
Save progress before leaving.

39
00:02:45.286 --> 00:02:51.045
For a course assignment. also check its own completion requirements; a free exploration is

40
00:02:51.045 --> 00:02:54.747
not automatically the same as finishing an assigned activity.
